{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-78506",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,1],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(-1)$ and $f(1).$\n\nSince $f(-1)=-2$ and $f(1)=4,$ we must have \n$k\\in [-2,4].$\n\nAmong the given options, only $k=3$ belongs to the interval $[-2,4].$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14270828/assets/d700db7d7cfbf5f34160f116a5d1f68d69be58bc2c38ce46fa24b5e6194670c6.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
